\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every $n$, can one choose coefficients $\varepsilon_k\in\{-1,1\}$ so that $P(z)=\sum_{k=1}^n\varepsilon_kz^k$ satisfies $\min_{|z|=1}|P(z)|>C_2\sqrt n$ for an absolute $C_2>0$? The stronger version asks for such a lower bound together with an upper bound $\max_{|z|=1}|P(z)|<C_1\sqrt n$ with an absolute $C_1$.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For every $n$, can one choose coefficients $\varepsilon_k\in\{-1,1\}$ so that $P(z)=\sum_{k=1}^n\varepsilon_kz^k$ satisfies $\min_{|z|=1}|P(z)|>C_2\sqrt n$ for an absolute $C_2>0$? The stronger version asks for such a lower bound together with an upper bound $\max_{|z|=1}|P(z)|<C_1\sqrt n$ with an absolute $C_1$.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 2304014 (AMR-022-4014). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
